# Erdős Problem #99 — outer-shadow forward chains force loaded slack or blocker contacts

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** Target C-II canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Claim class:** proof-only exact outer-walk refinement; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**External input:** none

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The low-`t` Type-II profile has two simultaneous metric features:

\[
P_F-q=\Theta(D),
\qquad
b-P_F\ll D.
\]

Thus the loaded force-hull vertices are sparse relative to hull perimeter, but the unit-contact outer walk shadows the long hull sides with little metric detour. This note makes that statement occurrence-exact.

Let

\[
d_{out}:=b-P_F\ge0,
\qquad
x_B:=b-q.
\]

There are at least

\[
\boxed{x_B-2d_{out}}
\tag{OS.1}

internal outer-walk occurrences whose incoming and outgoing unit edges both make **strictly positive projection** along the corresponding loaded hull side.

At every such occurrence, either

1. the source vertex is loaded of contact degree exactly two, contributing one unit to the loaded degree slack `s_B`; or
2. the source vertex has an additional actual positive contact edge besides the two walk edges.

If `E_{sh}` is the number of distinct chosen blocker edges needed to cover all source vertices of type 2, then

\[
\boxed{
2s_B+10E_{sh}
\ge x_B-2(b-P_F).
}
\tag{OS.2}
\]

This is finite and requires no small force-hull residual, no phase limit, and no Groemer stability.

At the AG58 safe low-`t` endpoint, Section 171 gives

\[
\boxed{
{2s_B+10E_{sh}\over D}
\ge1.30915411561035\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{OS.3}

Hence, after branch selection,

\[
\boxed{
{s_B\over D}\ge0.109096176300862\ldots-o(1)
}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
{E_{sh}\over D}\ge0.109096176300862\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{OS.4}

The weighted form (OS.3) is the real statement.

---

## 1. Exact projection decomposition on one loaded-hull interval

Use canonical Section-63 notation. Let

\[
W_i:b_i\leadsto b_{i+1}
\]

be the occurrence-counted outer contact-walk interval between consecutive loaded force-hull vertices, let

\[
s_i=|b_{i+1}-b_i|,
\qquad
u_i={b_{i+1}-b_i\over s_i},
\]

and orient the `ell_i` unit edge occurrences of `W_i` from `b_i` toward `b_{i+1}`. Write their oriented unit vectors as

\[
e_{i,1},\ldots,e_{i,\ell_i}.
\]

As an occurrence-counted walk,

\[
\sum_{j=1}^{\ell_i}e_{i,j}=s_i\nu_i.
\tag{1.1}
\]

Taking the scalar product with `nu_i`,

\[
\boxed{
\ell_i-s_i
=\sum_{j=1}^{\ell_i}(1-\nu_i\cdot e_{i,j}).
}
\tag{1.2}
\]

Every summand is nonnegative because `|e_{i,j}|=1`.

Summing over all loaded hull intervals gives exactly

\[
\boxed{
 b-P_F
 =\sum_{i,j}(1-\nu_i\cdot e_{i,j}).
}
\tag{1.3}
\]

This refines the metric inequality `b>=P_F`; the whole detour is a sum of nonnegative one-edge projection losses.

---

## 2. Nonforward edge occurrences are paid one-for-one

Call an edge occurrence `nonforward` when

\[
\nu_i\cdot e_{i,j}\le0.
\]

For such an occurrence,

\[
1-\nu_i\cdot e_{i,j}\ge1.
\]

Therefore, if `N_-` is the total number of nonforward outer edge occurrences,

\[
\boxed{N_-\le b-P_F.}
\tag{2.1}
\]

The number of internal vertex occurrences on all `W_i` is exactly

\[
\sum_i(\ell_i-1)=b-q=x_B.
\tag{2.2}
\]

Call an internal occurrence `forward-good` if both its incident walk-edge occurrences are forward, i.e. have positive projection along `nu_i`. One nonforward edge can spoil at most two internal occurrences. Hence

\[
\boxed{
G_{fw}\ge x_B-2N_-
\ge x_B-2(b-P_F).
}
\tag{2.3}

This proves (OS.1).

The statement remains valid with bridges, repeated articulation vertices, and repeated edge occurrences because everything is counted on the actual outer-face walk.

---

## 3. Local classification of a forward-good occurrence

Fix one forward-good internal occurrence at source vertex `v`. The incoming and outgoing walk edges are two **distinct contact incidences** at `v`: a degree-one bridge turnaround would traverse the same physical edge in opposite directions, and one of the two oriented occurrences would then have nonpositive projection, contrary to forward-goodness.

Thus `deg_C(v)>=2`.

If `deg_C(v)=2` and `v` is unloaded, positive contact equilibrium at `v` would require the two contact vectors to be exactly antiparallel with matching positive stresses. Canonical C26/C35 excludes unloaded positive-core degree-two vertices in C-II. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\deg_C(v)=2\Longrightarrow v\in B.
}
\tag{3.1}

Such a loaded degree-two vertex contributes exactly one to

\[
s_B=\sum_{b\in B}(3-\deg_Cb).
\]

Otherwise `deg_C(v)>=3`, so relative to the chosen outer-walk occurrence there exists at least one actual positive contact edge incident with `v` other than its two walk edges. Call one such edge a **shadow blocker** for `v`.

No stress lower bound is attached to this blocker.

---

## 4. Convert occurrences to distinct source currency

A vertex of degree `d` appears at most `d` times on the boundary walk of any fixed face: every occurrence uses an incident face sector, and there are only `d` sectors.

- A loaded degree-two vertex therefore supports at most two forward-good occurrences and contributes one unit to `s_B`.
- Every other positive-core vertex has degree at most five: unloaded vertices have degree at most five and loaded vertices at most three. Hence a vertex carrying a chosen blocker supports at most five forward-good occurrences.

Let `V_{blk}` be the number of distinct source vertices in the second class. Then

\[
G_{fw}\le2s_B+5V_{blk}.
\tag{4.1}
\]

Choose one blocker edge at each such source vertex, and let `E_{sh}` be the number of distinct chosen physical contact edges. One edge has only two endpoints, so it can be chosen by at most two blocker vertices. Therefore

\[
V_{blk}\le2E_{sh}.
\tag{4.2}
\]

Combining (2.3), (4.1), and (4.2) proves

\[
2s_B+10E_{sh}
\ge x_B-2(b-P_F).
\]

---

## 5. AG58 low-`t` specialization

On a convergent low-`t` Type-II subsequence put

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={b-P_F\over D}.
\]

Type II gives `P_F/D->pi`, so

\[
{x_B\over D}
={b-q\over D}
=\pi+\delta-t-\kappa+o(1).
\tag{5.1}
\]

AG58 and Section 171 give at the safe endpoint

\[
t=t_{58}=1.820922037979444\ldots
\]

and the exact necessary budget

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}
\le R_{58}:=0.00575825.
\tag{5.2}
\]

Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
{x_B-2(b-P_F)\over D}
&=\pi-t-\kappa-\delta+o(1)\\
&\ge\pi-t-2R_{58}-o(1)\\
&=1.30915411561035\ldots-o(1).
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.3}
\]

The minimization in the second line is exact: under (5.2), `kappa+delta` is maximized at `delta=0`, `kappa=2R58`.

Insert (5.3) into (OS.2) to obtain (OS.3)--(OS.4).

---

## 6. Relation to the small-residual pentagon theorem

The flat-pentagon middle theorem is stronger on the branch `R_F=o(D)`: it obtains a more structured population from cheap exceptional faces and forces

\[
s_B+2E_{block}
\ge0.4944931723\ldots D-o(D)
\]

near the AG58 floor.

The present theorem is different and broader:

- it does **not** assume `R_F=o(D)`;
- it uses only the global outer-shadow geometry;
- it identifies more than `1.309D` forward internal outer-walk occurrences before occurrence-to-source compression;
- its blockers arise from the near-geodesic hull-side shadow, not from Groemer pentagons.

Thus the residual-large branch still carries a substantial exact boundary-network obligation.

---

## 7. Scope and remaining obstruction

- `E_sh` counts actual positive unit-contact edges, not stress weight and not stopped trains.
- The chosen blocker at a source vertex may itself lie on another outer-face occurrence; no claim that it is geometrically inward is needed.
- The factor ten is deliberately conservative: five possible outer occurrences per blocker vertex and two blocker vertices per physical edge.
- Contact-isolated source points do not occur on the positive-contact outer walk and play no role.
- No packing capacity or reverse Oler statement is used.
- No exact unit triangle or strict sub-`D` competitor is produced.

The next useful theorem is to classify the blocker edges into singleton versus rhombic stopped trains and show that a linear blocker population cannot be absorbed without increasing one of the already-large train-defect currencies.

**Disposition:** proof-only exact outer-shadow theorem. C-II remains open.
